.TH sane-sp1130n 5 "3 November 2026" "@PACKAGEVERSION@" "SANE Scanner Access Now Easy"
.SH NAME
sane-sp1130n \- Ricoh/Fujitsu SP-1130N USB and native LAN backend
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B sp1130n
backend supports the Ricoh/Fujitsu SP-1130N over USB and native LAN.
Duplex scanning is the default. Front-only and back-only sources are available.
Basic hardware support has been tested on the Fujitsu-ID model; the Ricoh
USB ID and the limitations below have not all been physically validated.
Do not interpret synthetic test results as successful scans on real hardware.
.PP
For a focused build, select
.BR "BACKENDS=sp1130n" .
It is included in automatic backend selection when its Linux, libusb-1.0,
JPEG, pthread and C11 dependencies are available. The default dll.conf entry
is enabled. No vendor library or firmware is loaded.
.SH DEVICES
The accepted USB device IDs are Ricoh 05ca:031e and Fujitsu 04c5:1627.
Limited physical scanning tests used the Fujitsu ID; the Ricoh ID has not
been physically tested. Device names are
.BR sp1130n:usb:BUS:ADDRESS .
Use the name returned by scanimage \-L. The older non-N SP1130 and other Ricoh
devices are not inferred compatible.
.PP
Native LAN is selected explicitly as
.BR sp1130n:tcp:IPv4
or
.BR sp1130n:tcp:IPv4:PORT .
The default TCP port is 16888. Only numeric unicast IPv4 is accepted.
LAN is not enumerated, and no DNS lookup, broadcast, address change, eSCL or
WSD discovery is performed. Reported encryption is conservatively refused.
Plaintext transfer has no confidentiality or server authentication. Use a
trusted network and non-sensitive, replaceable originals for initial tests.
.SH OPTIONS
The source is ADF Duplex, ADF Front or ADF Back. Modes are Color, Gray or
Lineart. USB resolutions are 300 or 600 dpi; LAN is restricted to full A4 at
300 dpi with front/back output selected on the host. Duplex buffers both sides after one
sheet acquisition and returns the front image followed by the back image.
A client must use start/read/EOF/start/read/EOF without cancelling between sides.
Other resolutions, including the vendor driver's 1200-dpi output, are not
implemented. No host-side resolution resampling is performed.
.PP
Batch-scan defaults to yes and returns all sheets through successive starts.
When set to no, it consumes all loaded sheets but returns only the selected
side(s) of the first physical sheet. Later images are discarded on the host,
not saved. This is a frontend compatibility workaround, not single-sheet
feeding: do not load sheets that must remain in the tray.
The first start waits for feeder exhaustion and successful cleanup before
returning any image. Later-sheet faults or cancellation discard the retained
first sheet too. Draining is capped at 100 total sheets and checks a
120-second deadline between acquisitions; in-flight transactions retain their
existing bounded waits. Reaching either limit reports an error and may require
physical paper removal. Blank filtering applies only to the first sheet.
.PP
The upright-loading option defaults to no, matching documented face-down
loading and retaining native side/orientation handling.
For optional USB face-up loading with text readable upright in the tray,
set upright-loading to yes. Logical sides are swapped and both images
are rotated 180 degrees on the host. Acquisition-window coordinates are
mirrored to the native feeder orientation. Back-rotate applies an additional
rotation to the logical back only.
Upright-loading is false and inactive on LAN.
Sheet order remains acquisition order; the option does not change which sheet
the feeder picks first or reverse an entire stack.
.PP
Physical page dimensions and the tl-x, tl-y, br-x and br-y acquisition window
use integer millimeters on USB. These options are inactive for LAN.
Window coordinates are relative to the centered sheet; narrowing the physical
page clamps the window to its new edges. The nominal 216-mm maximum is bounded
to the feeder's pixel width (2550 pixels at 300 dpi, 5100 at 600 dpi).
Invalid or unsupported profiles are rejected before scanner I/O.
Brightness, contrast, threshold and back-rotate are computer-side processing.
Autocrop, deskew and skip-blank are optional and disabled by default. Blank
filtering may discard faint marks and changes front/back numbering. At most
32 fully blank sheets are acquired per start.
.PP
Rotation, autocrop, deskew and blank detection use SANE's shared sanei_magic
helpers. Autocrop detects paper edges rather than a content bounding box.
Crop-margin retains pixels around those edges. Deskew uses paper edges and
is limited to five degrees. If no suitable edges are found, geometry remains
unchanged. The former content-threshold option is no longer exposed.
Blank-per-mille limits average RGB darkness per thousand, not the number of
pixels below a threshold: zero means white and one thousand means black.
Blank detection follows deskew/crop and includes the entire resulting image,
including small scan windows and remaining edge marks.
Gray/Lineart conversion and brightness/contrast retain their
existing local implementation and output semantics.
Cancellation is checked between shared helper calls, not inside them.
.PP
LAN retains the image socket and client lock across sheets in one feeder job.
There is no prepare/start/stop cycle between sheets. Cancel requests termination
using lock-free atomic state; bounded cleanup occurs in a normal backend call,
on close, or in a per-handle worker polling every 100 ms while the frontend is
idle, never in a signal handler. Close waits for this worker.
USB finishes pending command/data
transactions and final completion records before cleanup. Its image phase has
a 120-second timeout budget, with up to thirty seconds for the first image
header and five seconds for later headers, and up to thirty seconds for a
payload transaction, followed by up to 25 seconds for STOP/unlock. Cancellation
can therefore wait for an accepted USB transaction; moving-paper cancellation
has not been physically validated.
After end-of-feeder, a paper jam or a failed job on either transport,
cancel or reopen before starting another job. Option changes during a live
job are refused.
.SH EXAMPLE
Replace the example address with the scanner's actual IPv4 address:
.PP
.nf
scanimage \\
  \-d sp1130n:tcp:192.168.1.50 \-\-source "ADF Duplex" \\
  \-\-batch=side-%03d.pnm \-\-batch-count=2 \-\-format=pnm
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
This backend is Linux-only and requires libusb-1.0, libjpeg and pthreads.
Only one SANE handle can be open. Discovery, encryption negotiation, scanner
configuration, vendor calibration, maintenance resets and firmware upload are
not implemented. Scanner faults or transport failure can leave a device lock
despite bounded cleanup. A frontend that cancels between pages loses the
buffered back image and ends the feeder job.
Opening a LAN handle is lazy and does not contact the scanner.
GNOME Document Scanner 46's Single Page action does this after one image,
even with Both Sides selected. It sets batch-scan to no, so the compatibility
mode consumes the entire loaded stack before returning that one image.
Use All Pages From Feeder with Both Sides to retain every duplex image,
including when only one sheet is loaded.
Its Image preset selects Color; the default Text preset selects Gray and
reduces the returned grayscale to four shades. Full eight-bit Gray and
one-bit Lineart remain available through clients exposing scan mode.
.PP
The USB transport uses SANE's shared sanei_usb helper.
It does not enable the previous direct-libusb inquiry/image-tail recovery:
the helper does not expose timeout-specific status or reliable partial-byte
counts on failed transfers. The current helper also clears endpoint halt
on bulk errors. A transfer failure blocks subsequent I/O on that connection,
including cleanup commands, rather than guessing the reply boundary.
No scanner reset or automatic acquisition retry is added.
Close/reopen clears host state, not necessarily pending replies or the
scanner lock; physical clearance or a power cycle may be needed after faults.
The shared helper passed one two-sheet Color/300-dpi duplex batch on
November 3, 2026, with four complete images, positive feeder exhaustion,
successful STOP/unlock, independent idle status and user-confirmed full
ejection. Other shared-USB modes and fault recovery remain unvalidated on
hardware; earlier direct-libusb results do not establish them.
.PP
Selected physical checks on a Fujitsu-ID SP-1130N passed native USB/LAN
Color/300-dpi labeled duplex, complete image output, positive exhaustion,
and loaded restart on one handle after exhaustion, cancel and reload.
USB front/back-only labels and optional back rotation passed limited tests.
LAN source-selection and back-rotation labels remain unverified.
Color/Gray/Lineart stacks also completed through localhost-only saned;
this is not GUI validation.
.PP
USB600 has both successful batches and unexplained intermittent startup
and partial-ejection failures. Complete images, empty-input status and
STOP/unlock acknowledgements do not prove physical paper ejection.
Calibrated windows/offsets, actual processing effects, moving-paper
cancellation, USB cover handling and broader fault recovery remain
unvalidated. Ethernet idle empty-cover rejection and fresh-handle recovery
have limited physical results; other recovery is not implied.
USB event-2 continuation (further block reads on the same START) and
alternate LAN START-prefix handling have synthetic coverage only.
Consecutive empty image replies are retried with a short backoff within
the existing acquisition deadline. See doc/sp1130n/README.md for the dated validation
summary and remaining review/release limits.
Short-header-only streams without a full side-identifying status record are
unsupported; front/back identity is never inferred from arrival order.
